# 7. Analytical “what next” command bank

The “what next” command bank lists user-facing analysis prompts across Activity Analysis, Training Load, Physiology, Performance Intelligence, Adaptation, Nutrition, Planning, Events & Performance, Forecasting, Optimization, System Explanations, and Data Exploration. These are not all direct one-to-one tool calls; they are intent-level routes that should call the appropriate report, activity, calendar, wellness, or performance-model tools as needed. 

## 7.1 Activity analysis family

**Canonical commands**
`analyze last activity`, `analyze training block`, `analyze performance trends`, `compare recent weeks`, `compare training phases`.

**Accepted aliases**
Natural variants: `review my last ride`, `compare this week to last week`, `analyze recent sessions`.

**User intent**
Understand execution and trend quality.

**Required inputs**
For single activity: activity ID or date, unless “last activity.”
For comparisons: date range, week references, or phase references.

**Optional inputs**
Sport, athlete ID.

**Default assumptions**
“Last activity” means latest completed activity. “Recent weeks” means runtime-reasonable recent history if UI does not supply dates.

**Data sources used**
Raw: `listActivitiesLight`, `getOneDayFullActivityV1`, reports when appropriate.
Montis interpretation: execution quality, fatigue, durability, repeatability, neural density.

**Calls involved**
`listActivitiesLight`, `getOneDayFullActivityV1`, optionally summary/weekly/season reports.

**Time window logic**
Single activity, one week vs another, or defined block.

**Key metrics**
Activity load, duration, zones, interval sequence, decoupling, W′, intensity density.

**Output structure**
Scope, raw findings, interpretation, coaching action.

**Read-only or write**
Read-only.

**Validation rules**
Do not compare undefined periods without resolving dates.

**Error cases**
No matching activities, missing sensor data, ambiguous phase.

**Clarifying questions**
“Which weeks or phases should I compare?”
“Which activity on that date?”

**Example prompts**
`analyze last activity`
`compare recent weeks`
`analyze performance trends`

**Example response outline**
“Scope: [range]. Trend: [summary]. Evidence: [metrics]. Interpretation: [Montis layer]. Recommendation: [action].”

